values ('df3f57e2-4c90-55ba-8db2-54649a4d7cd1', 'wg10-argument-writing-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-argument-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Saving the Ashby Hemlocks$q$, $q$(1) The hemlock trees in Ashby Town Forest shade the cold streams where native brook trout lay their eggs. (2) Over the past three years, an invasive sap-feeding insect has spread into the forest, and a survey by the Ashby Conservation Commission found that 40 percent of the hemlocks now show damage. (3) The commission should fund a treatment program that protects the 600 largest hemlocks along the forest's streams. (4) Without the hemlocks' shade, stream temperatures can rise several degrees in summer, which makes it harder for trout eggs to survive. (5) In nearby Thornbury Forest, where the same insect went untreated, nearly half the streamside hemlocks died within eight years. (6) Hiking in the forest is one of my favorite weekend activities.

(7) Some residents argue that the commission should simply let nature take its course. (8) They point out that forests have always changed and that the treatment would cost about $18,000 a year. (9) However, the insect is not part of the forest's natural history, since it arrived on nursery plants shipped from overseas. (10) The treatment would also be needed only until a beetle that feeds on the insect, now being tested at Marlowe State University, becomes available.

(11) Other residents suggest cutting the damaged trees and replanting with different species. (12) Young trees, however, would take decades to cast enough shade to cool the streams.

(13) In the end, the hemlocks are nice trees.$q$, 'draft')
