   $q$Read the sentence: "The podcast team recorded three episodes in one weekend; interviews with a mapmaker, a translator, and a bus driver, the editing took another two weeks." Which revision fixes the punctuation?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The podcast team recorded three episodes in one weekend; interviews with a mapmaker, a translator, and a bus driver; the editing took another two weeks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-before-phrase"},{"text":"The podcast team recorded three episodes in one weekend: interviews with a mapmaker, a translator, and a bus driver, the editing took another two weeks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"The podcast team recorded: three episodes in one weekend, interviews with a mapmaker, a translator, and a bus driver. The editing took another two weeks.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"colon-after-verb"},{"text":"The podcast team recorded three episodes in one weekend: interviews with a mapmaker, a translator, and a bus driver. The editing took another two weeks.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The podcast team recorded three episodes in one weekend: interviews with a mapmaker, a translator, and a bus driver. The editing took another two weeks.$q$, $q$A colon after "weekend" introduces the list of interviews, and a period then separates the list from the new clause "The editing took another two weeks." Keeping a comma before "the editing" still splices that independent clause onto the sentence.$q$, 'draft', null)
