insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('90d5b89a-687e-5974-9901-8028fa6c2b87', 'wg10-informative-writing-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Harvesting Ice from Frozen Lakes$q$, $q$(1) Long before electric refrigerators appeared in kitchens, families in northern towns kept food cold with blocks of ice cut from frozen lakes. (2) In the town of Brennick Falls, ice harvesting was the largest winter industry for more than fifty years. (3) My grandmother says that winters were much colder back then.

(4) Workers waited until the lake ice was at least 30 centimeters thick, which was strong enough to support horses and heavy sleds. (5) They first scraped away the snow and then marked the surface into a grid with an ice plow, a horse-drawn blade that cut deep grooves. (6) Next, crews used long saws to finish the cuts and floated the blocks along an open channel to the shore.

(7) The blocks were stored in icehouses, tall wooden buildings with thick double walls. (8) Workers packed sawdust between the walls and around every block. (9) The sawdust served as insulation, a material that slows the movement of heat, so ice cut in January could last until September. (10) According to records kept by the Brennick Falls Historical Society, a single large icehouse held about 9,000 tons of ice in 1890.

(11) By the 1930s, however, electric refrigerators had become affordable for many families. (12) Demand for natural ice dropped quickly, and the last icehouse in Brennick Falls closed its doors in 1941.$q$, 'draft')
