-- Passage wg10-informative-writing-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a9dedfbf-ccb8-5612-bb98-ec2ad6c46fa6', 'wg10-informative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-informative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Saving Old Maps$q$, $q$(1) In the basement of the Westover Public Library, conservator Lena Vasquez-Hale spends her days repairing maps that are more than two hundred years old. (2) Paper conservation is the careful work of stabilizing damaged documents so that they survive for future readers without changing their original appearance. (3) Unlike restoration, which may repaint faded areas or replace missing pieces, conservation aims only to preserve what remains.

(4) The greatest threat to old maps is acid. (5) Many papers made after about 1850 contain wood pulp, which breaks down over time and produces acids that turn pages yellow and brittle. (6) To slow this damage, conservators wash a map in a gentle water bath and then apply an alkaline solution that neutralizes the acid. (7) Tears are mended with thin tissue paper and a paste made from wheat starch.

(8) Every repair must be reversible, meaning a future conservator could remove it without harming the original paper. (9) "If a better method is discovered in fifty years, I want my work to come off cleanly," Vasquez-Hale explains. (10) The library also hosts a book club on Thursday evenings.

(11) Since the program began in 2016, the library has treated 312 maps, and 40 of them are now stable enough to be displayed in the main reading room.

(12) Maps are old.$q$, 'draft')
