-- Passage wg10-narrative-writing-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('eec1fa7f-011b-575b-ad08-2cbb6e10b9a0', 'wg10-narrative-writing-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-narrative-writing'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Tally Marks in the Snow$q$, $q$(1) On the coldest morning of December, my cousin Nadia and I volunteered for the town's annual winter bird count. (2) Our assigned area was a mile of trail along Fenwick Creek, and our job was to record every bird we saw or heard in three hours. (3) Nadia is twelve and had been looking forward to the count for weeks. (4) I had signed up mainly because it counted toward my community service hours.

(5) For the first hour we saw almost nothing. (6) The snow muffled every sound, and our boots crunched so loudly that I was sure we were scaring everything away. (7) Nadia kept stopping to listen with her eyes closed. (8) "There," she whispered, pointing at a bare oak. (9) I saw only branches until a small gray bird with a black cap dropped to a lower limb. (10) "Chickadee," she said, and made a careful tally mark in our notebook.

(11) After that, I started to notice things. (12) A flash of red in the brush became a cardinal. (13) A tapping sound turned out to be a downy woodpecker working along a dead branch. (14) Near the creek, a pair of ducks paddled through a gap in the ice, leaving dark ripples behind them. (15) My fingers went numb inside my gloves, but I no longer cared about the time.

(16) Snow is made of ice crystals that form around tiny bits of dust. (17) By noon our notebook held fourteen kinds of birds and sixty-two birds in all. (18) We handed it in at the nature center.$q$, 'draft')
