        '9-12', $q$When Railroads Set the Clocks$q$, $q$(1) Before the late 1800s, every town in the United States kept its own time, setting its clocks so that noon fell at the moment the sun stood highest overhead. (2) For farmers and shopkeepers, this local system worked well enough, since few people traveled far enough in a single day to notice the difference. (3) ____, the spread of railroads turned this patchwork of local times into a serious problem. (4) A train leaving one city might pass through dozens of towns, each with a slightly different idea of what time it was. (5) Some busy stations displayed several clocks side by side, each one showing the time used by a different rail company.

(6) Railroad managers eventually agreed on a solution that seems obvious today. (7) They divided the country into a small number of zones, each one hour apart, and every station within a zone would keep the same time. (8) On the chosen day in 1883, clocks in many towns were reset by a few minutes, and some residents joked that their town had experienced two noons in a single day. (9) Steam engines of that era burned large amounts of coal and wood. (10) ____, not every community welcomed the change, and a few local newspapers complained that private companies had no right to decide what time it was.

(11) Over the following decades, the zone system spread from the rails into ordinary life. (12) Schools, banks, and factories found that a shared time made schedules far easier to coordinate across long distances. (13) ____, the national government made the zones official law in 1918, long after most citizens had already adopted them in their daily routines.$q$, 'draft')
