   $q$Which of these longer sentences is complete rather than a fragment?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Because volunteers spent months matching the torn edges of the oldest letter in the archive, which a teenage sailor wrote in 1847.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"subordinate-clause-fragment"},{"text":"Volunteers who spent months matching the torn edges of the archive's oldest letter, which a teenage sailor wrote in 1847.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"relative-clause-fragment"},{"text":"The archive's oldest letter, written by a teenage sailor in 1847 and recently restored by volunteers who spent months matching its torn edges.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"participle-fragment"},{"text":"Although a teenage sailor wrote the archive's oldest letter in 1847, volunteers only recently restored it by matching its torn edges.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Although a teenage sailor wrote the archive's oldest letter in 1847, volunteers only recently restored it by matching its torn edges.$q$, $q$The "Although" sentence has a dependent clause followed by the main clause "volunteers only recently restored it," so it is complete. In the sentence beginning "Volunteers who spent months," every verb sits inside a relative clause, so "Volunteers" never gets a main verb.$q$, 'draft', null)
