insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('841f2565-2642-504f-afaa-fce168dbd8e2', 'wg10-writing-with-evidence-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g10-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: The Shade Map$q$, $q$(1) In her article "Hot Pavement, Cool Shade," geographer Amara Okafor reports on a summer study of sidewalk temperatures in the city of Millbrook. (2) Summer is the hottest season of the year in most places. (3) Okafor's team measured the same sidewalks at three in the afternoon on twenty clear days, comparing blocks with full tree cover to blocks with no trees at all. (4) She writes that shaded sidewalks were "on average nineteen degrees cooler to the touch than the bare concrete one street over." (5) This difference shows that tree cover changes the heat people actually feel, not just a number on a weather report.

(6) Okafor also points out who lives on the hottest blocks. (7) According to her map, the five neighborhoods with the least shade were also the ones with the fewest parks and the most bus stops without shelters. (8) ____, people waiting for buses in those areas stand on the hottest ground in the city for the longest time. (9) Okafor notes that her favorite tree in the study was a tall sycamore near the public library. (10) This pattern suggests that shade is not shared equally, and that planting trees could ease the heat where people need relief most.

(11) In conclusion, trees are nice to have on a street.$q$, 'draft')
